{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.MarkovWeightsConventionD","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovWeightsConventionD","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.markovWeightsD_barEven_combination","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.markovWeightsD_barEven_combination","kind":"theorem","line":116,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovWeightsConventionD.lean#L116-L130","doc":"**The prune, as a statement about what is available.**  Every D-frame\nweight is bar-even (the two above; the third is `0`, which is bar-even\ntrivially). So any `R`-linear combination of the D-frame weights is bar-even,\nand in particular no such combination supplies the bar-ODD eigenphase the\nC-frame ladder uses.\n\nStated as: if `f` is built from the weights by a map that commutes with\n`bar` — here, any linear combination with bar-even coefficients — then `f` is\nbar-even. The content is the two lemmas above; this records the conclusion\nthey are for. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.markovWeightsD_y3_barEven","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.markovWeightsD_y3_barEven","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovWeightsConventionD.lean#L109-L114","doc":"**`y_(3)` is bar-EVEN.**  `bar((q⁴+1)/(q²+1)²) = (q⁴+1)/(q²+1)²`, i.e.\n`(qi⁴+1)·(q²+1)² = (q⁴+1)·(qi²+1)²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.markovWeightsD_y21_barEven","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.markovWeightsD_y21_barEven","kind":"theorem","line":101,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovWeightsConventionD.lean#L101-L107","doc":"**`y_(2,1)` is bar-EVEN.**  `bar(q²/(q²+1)²) = q²/(q²+1)²`, in the\ncross-multiplied form `qi²·(qi²+1)⁻¹`-free: `qi² · (q²+1)² = q² · (qi²+1)²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.markovWeightsD_zsq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.markovWeightsD_zsq","kind":"theorem","line":87,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovWeightsConventionD.lean#L87-L92","doc":"**`z_D` squared is consistent with the cleared right-hand side above.**\n`(z·(q²+1))² = (q³)²` expands to `z²·W = q⁶`, matching\n`markovWeightsD_sigmasq`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.markovWeightsD_sigmasq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.markovWeightsD_sigmasq","kind":"theorem","line":82,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovWeightsConventionD.lean#L82-L85","doc":"**Second Markov equation** (`tr(σ²) = z²`), cleared by `W`.  The\n`y_(2,1)` eigenvalue is `−1` and `y_(1³)` drops, leaving `q⁶ = (z_D·W)²/W`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.markovWeightsD_sigma","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.markovWeightsD_sigma","kind":"theorem","line":76,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovWeightsConventionD.lean#L76-L80","doc":"**First Markov equation** (`tr(σ) = z`), cleared by `W`.  With\n`h = q − q⁻¹` the `q⁻¹` cancels: `(q − q⁻¹)·q² = q³ − q`, so the identity is\npolynomial. Right-hand side is `z_D·W = q³(q²+1)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.markovWeightsD_norm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.markovWeightsD_norm","kind":"theorem","line":70,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovWeightsConventionD.lean#L70-L74","doc":"**Normalisation.** `y_(3) + 2·y_(2,1) + y_(1³) = 1`, cleared: the three\nnumerators sum to `W = (q²+1)²`. Note `y_(1³)` contributes nothing — the\ntwo-row weights alone already saturate the normalisation. ","signatureChunk":0}]}